var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"07-a1b2c3d4","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Multi-AZ and Multi-Region","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs a critical REST API on EC2 instances behind an ALB in a single AZ. If that AZ fails, the API becomes unavailable. What is the MINIMUM change needed to make the API resilient to a single AZ failure?","options":{"A":"Add a second EC2 instance in the same AZ","B":"Enable Multi-AZ on the ALB and add EC2 instances in at least one additional AZ","C":"Create a CloudFront distribution in front of the ALB","D":"Enable enhanced networking on the existing EC2 instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ALBs are multi-AZ by nature — they automatically distribute traffic across healthy targets in multiple AZs. The minimum change for single-AZ resilience is: (1) ensure the ALB spans at least two AZs (ALB configuration), and (2) register EC2 instances in at least one additional AZ. If one AZ fails, the ALB routes all traffic to healthy instances in the surviving AZ.","distractors":{"A":"Adding a second instance in the same AZ improves within-AZ redundancy but provides no protection if the entire AZ fails.","C":"CloudFront adds edge caching and improves performance globally but does not make the origin resilient to AZ failures — if the AZ with the ALB fails, CloudFront has no healthy origin to send traffic to.","D":"Enhanced networking improves network throughput on EC2 instances but does not address AZ-level availability."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/application-load-balancers.html","tags":["alb","multi-az","resilience","ec2"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a1b2c3d5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Auto Scaling","difficulty":"medium","question":"An EC2 Auto Scaling group uses a target tracking policy on CPU utilization (target: 60%). During a traffic spike, new instances are launched but take 5 minutes to bootstrap (install software, configure). Users experience errors during the 5-minute warmup period. Which feature prevents the new instances from receiving traffic before they are ready?","options":{"A":"Set the cooldown period to 300 seconds","B":"Configure the Auto Scaling group's instance warmup period and use ALB health checks with a grace period","C":"Increase the minimum instance count to pre-warm instances","D":"Use EC2 Reserved Instances instead of On-Demand for faster launch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The instance warmup period tells Auto Scaling not to count the new instance's metrics toward the aggregate until it is warmed up. Combined with ALB health checks, the ALB only routes traffic to instances that pass health checks — which you define after the bootstrapping is complete (e.g., an HTTP 200 from /health). The health check grace period gives the instance time to complete bootstrapping before health checks begin.","distractors":{"A":"The cooldown period pauses Auto Scaling from launching more instances after a scaling activity — it does not prevent newly launched instances from receiving traffic before they are ready.","C":"A higher minimum increases baseline capacity but does not prevent existing OR new instances from failing to serve traffic during their bootstrapping window.","D":"Reserved Instances are a pricing commitment — they launch at the same speed as On-Demand instances and do not skip bootstrapping."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/ec2-auto-scaling-health-checks.html","tags":["auto-scaling","warmup","health-checks","alb","bootstrapping"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a1b2c3d6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"RDS and Aurora","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company uses RDS MySQL with Multi-AZ. The primary instance becomes unavailable due to a failure. How long does the automatic failover to the standby typically take?","options":{"A":"Instantaneous — RDS Multi-AZ uses active-active replication with no failover delay","B":"1-2 minutes — RDS detects the failure, promotes the standby, and updates the DNS endpoint","C":"15-30 minutes — the standby must apply all pending transaction logs before serving traffic","D":"24 hours — failover only occurs during the next maintenance window"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RDS Multi-AZ failover typically completes in 1-2 minutes. RDS detects the primary failure, promotes the synchronous standby replica to primary, and updates the CNAME record for the DB endpoint to point to the new primary. Applications reconnect using the same endpoint DNS name — no connection string changes needed. Applications experience a brief outage during the DNS TTL expiration and reconnection.","distractors":{"A":"Multi-AZ is not active-active — it uses a single primary with a synchronous standby. Failover involves DNS update and connection re-establishment, which takes 1-2 minutes.","C":"The standby is kept synchronously up-to-date — it does not need to apply pending logs. The 1-2 minute window is for failure detection and DNS propagation, not log replay.","D":"RDS Multi-AZ failover is automatic and occurs immediately upon failure detection — it does not wait for a maintenance window."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZSingleStandby.html","tags":["rds","multi-az","failover","rto"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a1b2c3d7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"SQS and decoupling","difficulty":"hard","question":"A processing pipeline uses SQS Standard Queue → Lambda. Lambda processes messages and writes results to DynamoDB. During a DynamoDB outage, Lambda fails with an error. What happens to the SQS messages?","options":{"A":"Messages are immediately sent to the DLQ after the first Lambda failure","B":"Lambda retries the batch — if it continues to fail, messages become visible again after the visibility timeout and are retried up to the maxReceiveCount before going to the DLQ","C":"Messages are permanently deleted from the queue when Lambda throws any exception","D":"SQS automatically pauses delivery during downstream outages and resumes when DynamoDB recovers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a Lambda function fails, SQS messages are not immediately deleted or sent to the DLQ. Lambda reports the batch failure to SQS, and SQS makes the messages visible again after the visibility timeout expires. SQS will re-attempt delivery up to the queue's \`maxReceiveCount\`. Only after exhausting the maxReceiveCount are messages moved to the configured Dead Letter Queue (DLQ). This provides automatic retry during transient outages like a temporary DynamoDB disruption.","distractors":{"A":"Messages go to the DLQ only after exhausting all retries (maxReceiveCount) — not after the first failure.","C":"SQS does not delete messages when Lambda throws exceptions — deletion only occurs after successful processing (Lambda deletes the message by returning success) or after the maxReceiveCount is exceeded.","D":"SQS does not have built-in awareness of downstream service health — it continues delivering messages to Lambda regardless of DynamoDB's state."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html","tags":["sqs","lambda","dlq","retry","outage"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a1b2c3d8","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Route 53 and DNS","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company wants to implement a disaster recovery strategy where a secondary region serves traffic ONLY if the primary region is unhealthy. Route 53 should automatically switch traffic. Which routing policy implements this?","options":{"A":"Weighted routing with primary=100, secondary=0","B":"Failover routing with health checks on the primary record","C":"Latency-based routing with health checks","D":"Geolocation routing with a default record in the secondary region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Route 53 Failover routing designates one record as PRIMARY and another as SECONDARY. Route 53 health checks continuously monitor the primary endpoint. When the primary health check fails, Route 53 automatically returns the secondary record in DNS responses, routing traffic to the secondary region. When the primary recovers, Route 53 automatically switches back. This is the canonical active-passive DR configuration.","distractors":{"A":"Weighted routing with 100/0 sends all traffic to the primary, but setting secondary weight to 0 means secondary never receives traffic — even on primary failure. Weighted routing does not support automatic health-check-based failover the same way failover routing does.","C":"Latency-based routing routes to the lowest-latency region — both regions receive traffic when healthy, not a true active-passive pattern.","D":"Geolocation routing routes based on user geographic location — it does not perform health-check-based automatic failover between primary and secondary."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/routing-policy-failover.html","tags":["route53","failover","health-checks","disaster-recovery"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a1b2c3d9","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"ElastiCache and caching","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company uses ElastiCache Redis in cluster mode with 3 shards. One shard's primary node fails. What happens to the Redis cluster?","options":{"A":"The entire cluster becomes unavailable until the failed node is replaced","B":"Redis cluster mode automatically promotes a replica to primary for the failed shard — the cluster remains available with a brief failover","C":"The two remaining shards continue serving their data; the failed shard's data is permanently lost","D":"ElastiCache automatically migrates the failed shard's slots to the two surviving shards"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ElastiCache Redis in cluster mode with replicas supports automatic failover. When a primary node fails, ElastiCache promotes one of the replica nodes for that shard to primary within seconds (typically 30-60 seconds). The cluster continues to serve reads from surviving shards immediately and resumes writes to the failed shard after the replica promotion completes. Cluster mode enables this sub-minute recovery for each independent shard.","distractors":{"A":"Redis cluster mode with replicas is designed to prevent full-cluster unavailability on single-node failures — only the specific shard's primary is briefly unavailable during failover.","C":"Data for the failed shard is not lost as long as a replica exists — the replica is promoted to primary and contains the same data as the failed primary (due to synchronous replication).","D":"ElastiCache does not migrate shard slots during a node failure — the shard's slot range remains assigned to that shard, and a replica is promoted to handle it."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/red-ug/AutoFailover.html","tags":["elasticache","redis","cluster-mode","failover","replica"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a2b3c4d5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"S3 and data durability","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company stores critical financial documents in S3 Standard. A developer accidentally deletes several thousand files using the AWS CLI. S3 versioning is NOT enabled. What is the recovery option?","options":{"A":"Restore from S3 Standard's built-in 30-day recycle bin","B":"Contact AWS Support to recover deleted objects from S3 infrastructure backups","C":"No recovery is possible — S3 Standard without versioning permanently deletes objects on DELETE requests","D":"Use S3 Inventory to find the deleted objects and restore them from Glacier"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without S3 versioning enabled, a DELETE request permanently removes the object — there is no native S3 recycle bin or recovery mechanism. S3 Glacier is a separate storage class (objects must be explicitly stored there); it is not an automatic backup of S3 Standard. The correct preventive measure is enabling S3 Versioning (DELETE requests create a 'delete marker', preserving the object version) or enabling S3 Object Lock. AWS does not restore accidentally deleted unversioned objects.","distractors":{"A":"S3 does not have a built-in recycle bin or trash folder for S3 Standard without versioning — deleted objects are gone immediately.","B":"AWS does not restore accidentally deleted customer objects from internal S3 infrastructure — the customer is responsible for enabling versioning or backups.","D":"S3 Inventory lists existing objects and their metadata — it cannot identify deleted objects. Glacier is not automatically populated from S3 Standard unless a lifecycle rule or manual archive was configured."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Versioning.html","tags":["s3","versioning","deletion","data-recovery","durability"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a2b3c4d6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"DynamoDB Global Tables","difficulty":"medium","question":"A gaming company uses DynamoDB Global Tables replicated in us-east-1 and eu-west-1. A player in Europe writes a score update. How does Global Tables handle the write?","options":{"A":"The write is synchronously committed to both regions before returning success to the client","B":"The write is committed locally in eu-west-1 and asynchronously replicated to us-east-1 using multi-active replication","C":"The write is routed to the us-east-1 primary region and then synchronously replicated to eu-west-1","D":"Global Tables uses eventual consistency — the client must poll until both regions confirm the write"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DynamoDB Global Tables uses active-active multi-master replication. Each region is a full replica that accepts reads and writes locally. A write committed in eu-west-1 is immediately visible in that region, then asynchronously replicated to us-east-1 (typically within 1 second). This eliminates cross-region write latency for European players. Conflict resolution uses 'last writer wins' based on timestamp.","distractors":{"A":"Global Tables replication is asynchronous — the write returns success to the client after committing locally, without waiting for replication to all regions.","C":"There is no designated primary region in Global Tables — it is active-active. Writes are not routed cross-region; they commit locally in the region where the request is received.","D":"The client receives a success response immediately after the local commit — no polling is required. Replication happens in the background."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GlobalTables.html","tags":["dynamodb","global-tables","active-active","replication","multi-region"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-a2b3c4d7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Load Balancing","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company's ALB has 10 EC2 targets across 2 AZs (6 in us-east-1a, 4 in us-east-1b). Cross-zone load balancing is disabled. What percentage of traffic does each instance in us-east-1b receive?","options":{"A":"10% — each of 10 instances receives equal traffic","B":"12.5% — traffic splits 50/50 between AZs; within us-east-1b, each of 4 instances gets 50%/4 = 12.5%","C":"10% in us-east-1a, 12.5% in us-east-1b","D":"6.67% in us-east-1a, 12.5% in us-east-1b"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Without cross-zone load balancing, each AZ's load balancer node distributes traffic only within its AZ. Traffic splits 50% to us-east-1a and 50% to us-east-1b. In us-east-1b with 4 instances, each instance receives 50% ÷ 4 = 12.5% of total traffic. In us-east-1a with 6 instances, each receives 50% ÷ 6 ≈ 8.33%. This creates unequal load — us-east-1b instances are each busier than us-east-1a instances. Cross-zone load balancing distributes evenly across all 10 (10% each).","distractors":{"A":"Equal 10% per instance only occurs with cross-zone load balancing enabled, which is not the case here.","C":"This describes the traffic from each instance's perspective but mixes up which AZ gets 10% and which gets 12.5%.","D":"6.67% per instance would occur in us-east-1a only with cross-zone load balancing enabled — without it, us-east-1a instances each get 8.33% (50%/6)."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/disable-cross-zone.html","tags":["alb","cross-zone","load-balancing","traffic-distribution"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b1c2d3e4","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM and permissions","difficulty":"easy","question":"A Lambda function needs to read from an S3 bucket and write to a DynamoDB table. How should the Lambda function be granted these permissions following the principle of least privilege?","options":{"A":"Hardcode AWS access keys (Access Key ID and Secret Access Key) in the Lambda function code","B":"Create an IAM role with policies granting S3 read and DynamoDB write permissions, and assign the role to the Lambda function","C":"Create an IAM user for the Lambda function and embed the credentials in environment variables","D":"Grant the Lambda function Administrator access for simplicity during development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The correct approach is to create an IAM role (execution role) with only the required permissions (S3 GetObject/ListBucket and DynamoDB PutItem/UpdateItem) and assign it to the Lambda function. Lambda automatically assumes the role at runtime using temporary credentials — no hardcoded keys needed. This follows the principle of least privilege and eliminates credential management overhead.","distractors":{"A":"Hardcoding credentials in code is a critical security vulnerability — credentials can be extracted from code repositories, logs, and binaries. AWS explicitly prohibits this practice.","C":"IAM users with static credentials stored in environment variables are still a security risk — credentials don't rotate automatically and can be exposed in logs.","D":"Administrator access violates the principle of least privilege — if the Lambda function is compromised, an attacker would have unrestricted access to all AWS services."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-intro-execution-role.html","tags":["iam","least-privilege","lambda","execution-role"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b1c2d3e5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"KMS and encryption","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company stores sensitive customer PII in S3 and requires that all data be encrypted at rest. The encryption keys must be generated and managed by the company, not AWS. Which S3 encryption option meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"SSE-S3 (Server-Side Encryption with S3-managed keys)","B":"SSE-KMS (Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS-managed keys)","C":"SSE-C (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-provided keys)","D":"Client-side encryption with a library like AWS Encryption SDK"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SSE-C (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys) allows customers to provide their own encryption keys with each S3 PUT and GET request. S3 uses the customer-provided key to encrypt/decrypt the object and does NOT store the key — only the key's HMAC is kept for validation. The customer manages the key entirely. Note: SSE-KMS uses KMS-managed keys where AWS stores key material in HSMs; SSE-C keeps keys purely under customer control.","distractors":{"A":"SSE-S3 uses keys generated and managed entirely by AWS — the customer has no control over or visibility into the keys.","B":"SSE-KMS uses CMKs stored in AWS KMS — while customers can control key policies and usage, the key material itself is managed by KMS (AWS infrastructure). Customer-managed KMS keys give control but AWS manages storage of the key material in HSMs.","D":"Client-side encryption (using AWS Encryption SDK) encrypts data before it reaches S3 and also meets the requirement, but SSE-C is the S3-native server-side option that most directly answers 'keys managed by the company, not AWS.'"},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerSideEncryptionCustomerKeys.html","tags":["s3","encryption","sse-c","kms","customer-managed"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b1c2d3e6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"VPC security","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's EC2 instance in a private subnet needs to access the AWS Secrets Manager API endpoint. The company prohibits any internet traffic from private subnets. Which solution enables access to Secrets Manager without internet connectivity?","options":{"A":"Create a NAT Gateway and route private subnet traffic through it to reach Secrets Manager","B":"Create a VPC Interface Endpoint for Secrets Manager in the VPC","C":"Bake all secrets into the AMI during instance launch","D":"Store secrets in S3 and use an S3 Gateway Endpoint instead"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Secrets Manager supports VPC Interface Endpoints (powered by AWS PrivateLink). Creating an interface endpoint in the VPC creates an elastic network interface with a private IP in the subnet. EC2 instances send API calls to Secrets Manager through this private endpoint — traffic never leaves the AWS network and no internet connectivity is required. The instance's code uses the same Secrets Manager SDK; only the endpoint URL changes (or is resolved automatically via private DNS).","distractors":{"A":"A NAT Gateway routes traffic to the internet — this violates the requirement prohibiting internet traffic from private subnets.","C":"Baking secrets into an AMI is a security anti-pattern — secrets are embedded in the machine image, making rotation difficult and exposing them to anyone with AMI access.","D":"S3 is an object store, not a secrets management service. Storing secrets in S3 lacks rotation, audit trails, and access control features. An S3 Gateway Endpoint connects to S3, not Secrets Manager."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/vpc-endpoint-overview.html","tags":["secrets-manager","vpc-endpoint","privatelink","private-subnet","security"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b1c2d3e7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"WAF and Shield","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's public-facing API is experiencing a DDoS attack — thousands of bot IPs are sending 10,000 requests per second each, exhausting the API Gateway throttle limits. AWS Shield Standard is already enabled. What additional service protects against this sophisticated L7 DDoS attack?","options":{"A":"Enable AWS Shield Advanced on the API Gateway — it provides automatic L7 DDoS mitigation","B":"Deploy AWS WAF with rate-based rules and IP reputation lists in front of the API Gateway","C":"Increase API Gateway throttle limits to handle the higher request volume","D":"Enable VPC Flow Logs to identify and manually block attacking IPs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS WAF with rate-based rules automatically blocks IPs that exceed a configurable request rate (e.g., block any IP sending > 1,000 requests per 5 minutes). AWS Managed Rules for AWS WAF include IP reputation lists and bot control that identify and block known malicious IPs and bot traffic. WAF operates at Layer 7 and can inspect request patterns, headers, and body — providing intelligent L7 DDoS mitigation that Shield Standard does not offer.","distractors":{"A":"AWS Shield Advanced provides enhanced DDoS protection, cost protection, and 24/7 DRT access, but the primary L7 application-layer protection tool is WAF — Shield Advanced is best combined with WAF, not instead of it.","C":"Increasing throttle limits allows more attack traffic through — it does not block the attack and increases costs.","D":"VPC Flow Logs provide traffic visibility but cannot block traffic — manually reviewing logs during an active attack is too slow to be effective."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-rate-based-rules.html","tags":["waf","ddos","shield","rate-based-rules","api-gateway"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b1c2d3e8","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Cognito","difficulty":"medium","question":"A mobile app allows users to log in with Google or Facebook. After login, users need to access an S3 bucket with per-user permissions (each user can only access their own folder). Which AWS service combination enables federated social login AND scoped S3 access?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito User Pool for social login + Cognito Identity Pool to exchange tokens for temporary AWS credentials","B":"IAM Identity Center for social federation + IAM roles for S3 access","C":"API Gateway with a Lambda authorizer validating Google/Facebook tokens","D":"AWS Directory Service with social login federation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cognito User Pools handle authentication with social identity providers (Google, Facebook) using OAuth 2.0/OIDC federation. After authentication, the User Pool token is exchanged in a Cognito Identity Pool for temporary AWS credentials (via STS AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity). The IAM role for authenticated users can include a policy condition using \`cognito-identity.amazonaws.com:sub\` to restrict each user to their own S3 prefix — providing per-user scoped access.","distractors":{"B":"IAM Identity Center is designed for workforce identity (employee SSO to AWS accounts and business applications) — not for consumer mobile app users authenticating with Google/Facebook.","C":"API Gateway with Lambda authorizer can validate social tokens, but this only secures the API — it does not provide users with direct S3 access credentials. A separate mechanism would still be needed for S3.","D":"AWS Directory Service is for Active Directory and LDAP directory services — it does not natively support consumer social identity providers like Google and Facebook."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/cognito-identity.html","tags":["cognito","identity-pool","user-pool","federation","s3","mobile"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b1c2d3e9","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"GuardDuty and Inspector","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company wants continuous threat detection that automatically analyzes CloudTrail API logs, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS query logs to identify unauthorized behavior, credential compromise, and unusual API calls. Which AWS service provides this?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty is a managed threat detection service that continuously ingests and analyzes CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs using machine learning, anomaly detection, and threat intelligence feeds. It generates findings for events like unusual API calls, crypto mining activity, credential exfiltration, and communication with known malicious IPs — without requiring any configuration of what to look for.","distractors":{"A":"AWS Config tracks resource configurations and evaluates compliance against rules — it does not analyze behavioral data (API call patterns, network flows) for threat detection.","C":"AWS Security Hub aggregates, normalizes, and prioritizes findings from multiple security services (including GuardDuty) — it is a security posture dashboard, not a threat detector itself.","D":"Amazon Inspector is a vulnerability assessment service for EC2 and container images — it identifies software vulnerabilities and CVEs, not behavioral threat patterns in logs."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/guardduty/latest/ug/what-is-guardduty.html","tags":["guardduty","threat-detection","cloudtrail","vpc-flow-logs","security"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b2c3d4e5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Organizations and SCPs","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company uses AWS Organizations with SCPs. An SCP at the root OU denies all S3 DeleteObject actions. An account administrator has an IAM policy granting s3:* (all S3 actions). Can the administrator delete objects in S3?","options":{"A":"Yes — IAM policies take precedence over SCPs at the account level","B":"No — SCPs are guardrails; the effective permission is the intersection of IAM policies and SCPs. If SCP denies DeleteObject, no IAM policy can grant it","C":"Yes — explicit ALLOW in IAM policy overrides explicit DENY in SCP","D":"It depends on whether the SCP is applied to the root OU or a child OU"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SCPs are guardrails that define the maximum permissions available in an account. The effective permission for any principal is the intersection of what the SCP allows AND what the IAM policy allows. If the SCP denies s3:DeleteObject, no IAM policy — including Administrator or s3:* — can grant that action within the account. SCPs take precedence over IAM policies by restricting what actions are even possible, regardless of IAM grants.","distractors":{"A":"IAM policies do NOT take precedence over SCPs — SCPs constrain what IAM can grant. An IAM allow cannot overcome an SCP deny.","C":"IAM explicit ALLOWs cannot override SCP explicit DENYs — the permission evaluation order is: SCP deny → IAM deny → SCP allow → IAM allow. An SCP deny terminates evaluation immediately.","D":"The SCP applies to all accounts under the OU — the account's position doesn't affect whether the SCP deny is effective once applied. A deny SCP at root applies to all child OUs and accounts."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html","tags":["scp","organizations","iam","permissions","guardrails"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b2c3d4e6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"Secrets Manager","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company rotates database credentials using AWS Secrets Manager automatic rotation. After rotation, some application pods still use the old credentials and fail to connect. What is the most likely cause and fix?","options":{"A":"Secrets Manager rotation corrupted the credentials — disable automatic rotation","B":"Application pods cache the secret value at startup and do not re-fetch it after rotation — fix by fetching the secret dynamically from Secrets Manager on each connection or on cache miss","C":"Secrets Manager only rotates credentials in one region — enable multi-region secrets","D":"The rotation Lambda function needs to be in the same VPC as the database"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Application pods that read the secret once at startup (e.g., into a class variable or environment variable) cache the old credentials. After rotation, they continue using the cached value until restarted. The fix is to implement dynamic secret retrieval — fetch the secret from Secrets Manager with each connection, or implement a cache with TTL that refreshes before rotation occurs. AWS recommends caching secrets with a TTL shorter than the rotation interval.","distractors":{"A":"Automatic rotation is a feature, not a bug — it correctly rotates the database password. The issue is application-side caching, not rotation failure.","C":"Multi-region secrets replicate secrets across regions — they do not affect whether application pods refresh cached credentials.","D":"The rotation Lambda does need network access to the database (which may require VPC configuration), but if rotation succeeded and the new password works, this is not the cause of the caching issue."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html","tags":["secrets-manager","rotation","caching","credentials","application"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b2c3d4e7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"ACM and HTTPS","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company wants to serve their website over HTTPS using a certificate managed by AWS. They want automatic renewal without manual intervention. Which service provides this?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM server certificate upload","B":"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) with a public certificate","C":"Self-signed certificate stored in S3","D":"Let's Encrypt certificate manually renewed every 90 days"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) issues and manages public SSL/TLS certificates for use with ALB, CloudFront, API Gateway, and other AWS services. ACM automatically renews certificates before expiration (approximately 60 days before expiry) with no manual intervention required. Public certificates from ACM are free. Certificates cannot be exported from ACM (they are deployed directly to AWS services).","distractors":{"A":"IAM server certificates are for uploading externally obtained certificates to use with older AWS services (primarily legacy ELB) — IAM does not issue certificates and does not provide automatic renewal.","C":"Self-signed certificates are not trusted by browsers and require manual installation and renewal — not appropriate for public-facing websites.","D":"Let's Encrypt provides free certificates with 90-day validity but requires automated ACME client renewal tooling (Certbot) — this is self-managed, not AWS-managed."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/acm/latest/userguide/acm-overview.html","tags":["acm","https","certificate","renewal","ssl"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b2c3d4e8","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"S3 security","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company stores sensitive data in S3 with bucket versioning enabled. A compliance requirement states that objects must not be deleted for 7 years. Even the root account must be unable to delete objects during this period. Which S3 feature enforces this immutability?","options":{"A":"S3 Bucket Policy with a Deny for DeleteObject","B":"S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode with a 7-year retention period","C":"S3 MFA Delete requiring a physical MFA token for deletions","D":"AWS Config rule that detects and alerts on S3 deletions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode prevents ANY user — including the AWS root account — from deleting or overwriting locked objects before the retention period expires. The compliance mode retention period cannot be shortened or the lock removed by any user. This provides WORM (Write Once, Read Many) storage that satisfies strict regulatory requirements like SEC 17a-4. Governance mode is similar but allows privileged users (with s3:BypassGovernanceRetention) to override.","distractors":{"A":"Bucket policies can be modified or deleted by anyone with sufficient IAM permissions — including account administrators and the root user. A bucket policy deny is not immutable.","C":"MFA Delete requires an MFA token for permanent DELETE requests and changing versioning state — but the root account can always disable MFA Delete. It does not create time-locked retention.","D":"AWS Config rules detect and alert on configuration changes — they are reactive. By the time Config detects a deletion, the object is already gone. Config cannot enforce immutability proactively."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-overview.html","tags":["s3","object-lock","compliance","worm","immutability"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-b2c3d4e9","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"CloudTrail","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company wants an immutable audit trail of all AWS API activity across 12 accounts in AWS Organizations. They want the logs to be centralized and tamper-proof. Which configuration achieves this?","options":{"A":"Enable CloudTrail in each account individually and store logs in separate account-specific S3 buckets","B":"Create an Organization Trail in the management account that delivers logs from all accounts to a centralized S3 bucket in a dedicated logging account with Object Lock enabled","C":"Use AWS Config aggregator to collect API call data across all accounts","D":"Enable VPC Flow Logs in each account and consolidate in CloudWatch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An AWS Organizations Organization Trail automatically enables CloudTrail logging in all current and future member accounts. Logs are delivered to a centralized S3 bucket (ideally in a separate, dedicated logging account with restricted access). Enabling S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode on the logging bucket creates a tamper-proof, immutable audit trail — even the member account administrators cannot delete the logs. CloudTrail log file integrity validation further detects any tampering.","distractors":{"A":"Individual account trails create fragmented logging across 12 buckets — harder to audit holistically and each account's admin can potentially delete their own trail.","C":"AWS Config aggregator collects resource configuration data and compliance findings — it does not capture API call history (CloudTrail data).","D":"VPC Flow Logs capture network traffic data — they record source/destination IPs and ports, not AWS API calls (who called what API, when, with what parameters)."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/creating-trail-organization.html","tags":["cloudtrail","organizations","audit","s3","object-lock","centralized"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-c1d2e3f4","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"EC2 and compute","difficulty":"medium","question":"An application processes large scientific datasets. Each job needs 192 vCPUs and 1.5 TB of RAM running for 2-4 hours. No single EC2 instance provides this specification. What approach meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use an HPC cluster with EC2 instances connected via Elastic Fabric Adapter (EFA) for tightly coupled parallel processing","B":"Run the job on a single x2iedn.32xlarge (128 vCPUs, 4 TB RAM)","C":"Use Amazon EMR with Spark to distribute processing across multiple standard instances","D":"Submit the job to AWS Batch with a multi-node parallel job definition using multiple instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Batch supports Multi-node Parallel (MNP) jobs that span multiple EC2 instances, appearing as a single logical job. AWS Batch manages instance provisioning, job scheduling, and inter-node communication. For a job requiring 192 vCPUs (e.g., 3× c5.24xlarge with 96 vCPUs each), Batch launches coordinated instances, runs the distributed application, and terminates instances when done — fully managed with automatic scaling.","distractors":{"A":"EFA-connected HPC clusters enable MPI-style tightly coupled applications with ultra-low latency — this is valid for HPC simulations but requires application code written for MPI/parallel computing frameworks.","B":"x2iedn.32xlarge provides 128 vCPUs and 4 TB RAM — not 192 vCPUs. No single instance provides exactly 192 vCPUs and 1.5 TB RAM in the standard catalog.","C":"EMR with Spark distributes MapReduce-style batch processing — it requires rewriting the application as a Spark job and has overhead not suited for tightly coupled scientific computations."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/batch/latest/userguide/multi-node-parallel-jobs.html","tags":["batch","hpc","multi-node","compute","distributed"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-c1d2e3f5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Lambda and serverless","difficulty":"medium","question":"A REST API built with API Gateway and Lambda processes file uploads up to 50 MB. Users complain that uploads time out. What is the root cause?","options":{"A":"API Gateway has a maximum payload size of 10 MB and a 29-second integration timeout","B":"Lambda has a maximum function duration of 60 seconds","C":"S3 does not accept files over 5 MB via Lambda","D":"API Gateway HTTPS requires TLS 1.3 which adds overhead"},"correct":"A","explanation":"API Gateway REST APIs have two hard limits relevant here: (1) maximum payload size of 10 MB (request and response) — a 50 MB upload exceeds this limit. (2) maximum integration timeout of 29 seconds. The solution for large file uploads is to use a pre-signed S3 URL: the client requests a pre-signed URL from the API, then uploads directly to S3 bypassing API Gateway — S3 supports files up to 5 TB (or 160 GB via single PUT).","distractors":{"B":"Lambda's maximum execution duration is 15 minutes (900 seconds), not 60 seconds — but the actual issue is API Gateway's 10 MB payload limit, not Lambda duration.","C":"S3 accepts files up to 5 GB via a single PUT and up to 5 TB via multipart upload — there is no 5 MB S3 limit.","D":"TLS overhead is milliseconds — it does not cause upload timeouts for file size issues."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/limits.html","tags":["api-gateway","lambda","payload","s3","presigned-url","limits"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-c1d2e3f6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"CloudFront and CDN","difficulty":"medium","question":"A video streaming platform uses CloudFront to deliver HLS video content. To prevent users from sharing stream URLs, each video URL should be valid for only 24 hours and tied to the authenticated user. What CloudFront feature achieves this?","options":{"A":"CloudFront geographic restrictions","B":"CloudFront signed cookies with a custom policy specifying 24-hour expiration","C":"S3 Block Public Access enabled on the origin bucket","D":"CloudFront WAF rule matching authorized user-agent strings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CloudFront signed cookies work like signed URLs but set the access credentials in browser cookies rather than URL parameters. With a custom policy, you specify an expiry time (24 hours), the resource pattern (e.g., all HLS segments for a video), and optionally IP restriction. This is ideal for HLS video where multiple .ts segment files and .m3u8 manifest files need authorization — a signed cookie covers all segments without embedding signatures in each URL.","distractors":{"A":"Geographic restrictions block access by country/location — they do not implement per-user 24-hour access expiry or prevent URL sharing.","C":"S3 Block Public Access prevents public direct S3 access but does not restrict CloudFront-served content per user or implement time-based expiry.","D":"User-agent strings can be easily spoofed — they do not provide cryptographic authentication and can be copied along with the URL by any user."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-signed-cookies.html","tags":["cloudfront","signed-cookies","video-streaming","access-control","hls"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-c1d2e3f7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"DynamoDB","difficulty":"hard","question":"A DynamoDB table has a partition key \`userId\` and sort key \`timestamp\`. A query returns 1,000 items but the application only needs items where \`status = 'active'\` (roughly 10% of results). How does DynamoDB apply the filter and what are the cost implications?","options":{"A":"DynamoDB only reads items matching the filter — a FilterExpression reduces both RCU consumption and returned items","B":"DynamoDB reads all 1,000 items (consuming full RCUs), applies the filter server-side, and returns ~100 items — RCU cost is based on all items read, not filtered results","C":"FilterExpression is only valid for Scan operations, not Query operations","D":"DynamoDB automatically creates a secondary index when a FilterExpression is frequently used"},"correct":"B","explanation":"FilterExpression in DynamoDB Query operations is applied AFTER the items are read and the read capacity is consumed. All 1,000 items matching the key condition are read (consuming RCUs for all 1,000 items), then the FilterExpression discards 900 of them server-side. You pay for reading all 1,000 items. For efficiency, use GSI or composite sort keys to express the filter as a key condition instead — key conditions are evaluated during the read, reducing RCU consumption.","distractors":{"A":"FilterExpression does NOT reduce RCU consumption — all items matching the key condition are read first. The filter only reduces the data returned to the caller, not the capacity consumed.","C":"FilterExpression works for both Query AND Scan operations in DynamoDB.","D":"DynamoDB does not automatically create secondary indexes — you must design and create GSIs explicitly."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.FilterExpression.html","tags":["dynamodb","filter-expression","rcu","query","cost"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-c1d2e3f8","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Kinesis and streaming","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company uses Kinesis Data Streams for real-time event processing. The stream has 5 shards. A consumer using the GetRecords API (standard polling) reads from all 5 shards. Another team wants to add a second independent consumer reading the same stream. What is the read throughput concern?","options":{"A":"Two consumers cannot read from the same Kinesis stream simultaneously","B":"Standard consumers share the 2 MB/s per shard read throughput — with 2 consumers, each gets only 1 MB/s per shard","C":"The second consumer must use a different starting position (TRIM_HORIZON vs LATEST)","D":"Adding a second consumer doubles the Kinesis stream cost"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Standard Kinesis consumers using GetRecords share the per-shard read throughput of 2 MB/s and 5 GetRecords calls per second. With two consumers each polling the same shard, they compete for the same 2 MB/s bandwidth. For multiple consumers each needing the full 2 MB/s per shard, use Kinesis Enhanced Fan-Out — it provides dedicated 2 MB/s per consumer per shard using a push model (SubscribeToShard API), eliminating read throughput contention.","distractors":{"A":"Multiple consumers CAN read from the same Kinesis stream — standard GetRecords and Enhanced Fan-Out both support multiple independent consumers.","C":"Starting position (TRIM_HORIZON, LATEST, AT_TIMESTAMP) determines where in the stream a new consumer begins reading — it is unrelated to the throughput limitation between multiple consumers.","D":"Kinesis Data Streams pricing is based on shard hours and data ingested — adding a second consumer increases GetRecords API call costs but does not double the stream cost."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/enhanced-consumers.html","tags":["kinesis","enhanced-fan-out","consumers","throughput","read-bandwidth"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-c2d3e4f5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Aurora and RDS","difficulty":"medium","question":"An Aurora MySQL cluster has one writer and 5 reader instances. The application uses the cluster endpoint for writes and the reader endpoint for reads. During a failover event where the writer fails, what happens to the reader endpoint?","options":{"A":"The reader endpoint becomes unavailable until a new writer is promoted","B":"The reader endpoint continues to work — it automatically excludes the instance being promoted (briefly unavailable) and routes to other readers","C":"The reader endpoint routes all reads to the new writer until the old readers are reconfigured","D":"The reader endpoint must be manually updated to point to the remaining readers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aurora's reader endpoint is managed by Aurora and automatically tracks healthy reader instances. During failover, one reader is promoted to writer — it is briefly unavailable as the primary during the transition. The Aurora reader endpoint automatically excludes the instance being promoted and continues routing reads to the remaining 4 healthy reader instances. After the new writer is established, Aurora adjusts the reader endpoint to include the remaining readers.","distractors":{"A":"The reader endpoint does not become unavailable — it excludes the transitioning instance and continues serving reads from other readers.","C":"The writer endpoint points to the new primary (formerly a reader) — the reader endpoint continues routing to remaining read-only instances, not the new writer.","D":"Aurora cluster and reader endpoints are fully managed — they automatically track topology changes without manual intervention."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Aurora.Overview.Endpoints.html","tags":["aurora","failover","reader-endpoint","cluster","high-availability"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-d1e2f3g4","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"EC2 cost optimization","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company runs a development environment with 20 EC2 instances that are only needed Monday-Friday 8 AM to 6 PM (UTC). The instances run 24/7 currently. Which approach reduces cost by the maximum amount?","options":{"A":"Convert all instances to Reserved Instances for 1 year","B":"Use Instance Scheduler or a Lambda/EventBridge solution to automatically stop instances outside business hours — reducing runtime from 168 hours/week to 50 hours/week","C":"Use Spot Instances for all 20 development instances","D":"Switch to AWS Fargate instead of EC2 for development workloads"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Development instances only needed 50 hours/week (Mon-Fri, 8am-6pm = 10 hours × 5 days) versus 168 hours/week currently. Stopping instances outside business hours reduces compute costs by approximately 70% (50/168). AWS Instance Scheduler (or a custom Lambda + EventBridge scheduled rule) automates start/stop. This is often the biggest single cost optimization for dev/test environments and outperforms Reserved Instances (which provide ~40-72% savings but still run 24/7).","distractors":{"A":"Reserved Instances provide 40-72% savings on the hourly rate but instances still run 168 hours/week. Stopping instances for 70% of the time saves more than any RI discount.","C":"Spot Instances provide up to 90% savings on hourly rate but can be interrupted — suitable for fault-tolerant workloads, not interactive development environments where interruption disrupts work.","D":"Switching to Fargate changes the compute platform but doesn't address the fundamental issue of running 24/7 when only needed 50 hours/week."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/latest/instance-scheduler-on-aws/solution-overview.html","tags":["ec2","cost","scheduling","development","stop-start"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-d1e2f3g5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"S3 and storage cost","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company pays $500/month in S3 costs. S3 Cost Explorer shows 40% of the bill is from S3 API requests (PUT, GET, LIST). The company makes millions of small LIST operations on a bucket with 10 billion objects to find recent files. Which change reduces API costs?","options":{"A":"Switch to S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically reduce API costs","B":"Enable S3 Inventory to generate daily/weekly object lists, reducing the need for real-time LIST API calls","C":"Move objects to S3 Glacier to reduce API request rates","D":"Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration to reduce API latency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"S3 LIST operations on buckets with billions of objects are expensive — each LIST returns up to 1,000 objects and charges per request. S3 Inventory generates daily or weekly CSV/ORC/Parquet reports of all objects in a bucket (stored in S3). Applications can query the inventory file (e.g., with Athena) to find recent files instead of issuing thousands of LIST API calls. Inventory charges a flat fee per million objects listed — far cheaper than millions of individual LIST calls.","distractors":{"A":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering optimizes storage costs based on access patterns — it does not reduce the number or cost of API requests made by the application.","C":"Moving objects to Glacier changes storage class for rarely-accessed data — it does not reduce LIST API costs for active objects and adds retrieval costs when files are needed.","D":"Transfer Acceleration improves upload speeds to S3 using CloudFront edges — it increases cost (additional per-GB fee) and does not reduce LIST API call frequency."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-inventory.html","tags":["s3","inventory","api-costs","list","cost-optimization"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-d1e2f3g6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Serverless cost","difficulty":"easy","question":"A company migrates from EC2 (m5.large, 24/7 = $69.12/month On-Demand) to a Lambda function. The Lambda (512 MB) runs for 200ms per invocation, 1 million times per month. What is the approximate monthly Lambda cost?","options":{"A":"$0 — within the free tier","B":"$0.60 — ($0.20/million requests) + ($0.0000001667 × 0.5 GB × 0.2s × 1M invocations)","C":"$69.12 — Lambda costs the same as EC2","D":"$100 — Lambda is more expensive than EC2 for high-invocation workloads"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lambda cost = requests + duration. Requests: 1M × $0.20/1M = $0.20. Duration: 512 MB = 0.5 GB-seconds per second × 0.2 seconds = 0.1 GB-second per invocation × 1M = 100,000 GB-seconds × $0.0000166667 = $1.67. Total ≈ $1.87/month. Well under the EC2 cost of $69.12/month. Free tier (1M requests + 400K GB-seconds/month) may make this even cheaper. Lambda is dramatically more cost-effective for this invocation pattern.","distractors":{"A":"While the free tier covers 1M requests, the 100,000 GB-seconds of duration exceeds the 400,000 GB-seconds free tier in this example — cost is minimal but not zero.","C":"Lambda pricing is fundamentally different from EC2 — EC2 charges per-hour regardless of usage, Lambda charges per-invocation and per-duration only when running.","D":"Lambda is significantly cheaper than EC2 for this workload — 1M × 200ms × 512MB is a modest compute footprint compared to a continuously running EC2 instance."},"source_url":"https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/pricing/","tags":["lambda","pricing","cost","serverless","ec2-comparison"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-d1e2f3g7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Data transfer and NAT","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company's monthly AWS bill shows $800 in NAT Gateway data processing fees. Analysis shows most traffic is EC2 instances in private subnets downloading packages from the internet (apt, pip, npm) during deployments. Which architectural change eliminates MOST of this cost?","options":{"A":"Increase NAT Gateway size to a larger instance type","B":"Use AWS CodeArtifact or a private package repository in the VPC to cache packages, eliminating internet downloads via NAT","C":"Move all EC2 instances to public subnets","D":"Use VPC endpoints for all package downloads"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CodeArtifact (for npm, pip, Maven) or a private package repository (Nexus, Artifactory) in the VPC caches packages locally. EC2 instances download packages from the internal repository (no internet, no NAT) instead of from public package registries. This eliminates the NAT Gateway data processing charges ($0.045/GB) for package downloads. AWS CodeArtifact can also serve as an upstream proxy that caches packages from public registries on first access.","distractors":{"A":"NAT Gateway is a managed service with no 'instance types' or sizes to upgrade — you pay per GB processed regardless.","C":"Moving to public subnets eliminates NAT but exposes instances directly to the internet, violating security best practices and potentially increasing attack surface.","D":"VPC endpoints only exist for AWS services (S3, DynamoDB, etc.) — public package registries (pypi.org, npmjs.com) do not have VPC endpoints."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/codeartifact/latest/ug/welcome.html","tags":["nat-gateway","codeartifact","cost","packages","data-transfer"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-d1e2f3g8","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Reserved Instances and Savings Plans","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company has 100 m5.xlarge On-Demand EC2 instances running 24/7. They want to optimize costs with a 1-year commitment. They expect to change instance types within the m-family over the next year. Which commitment type provides the best savings while maintaining flexibility to change instance size?","options":{"A":"1-year Standard Reserved Instances for m5.xlarge","B":"1-year EC2 Instance Savings Plans for the m-family in us-east-1","C":"1-year Compute Savings Plans","D":"Convertible Reserved Instances for m5.xlarge"},"correct":"B","explanation":"EC2 Instance Savings Plans provide up to 72% savings (same as Standard RIs) for a specific instance family (m-family) and region. Within that family, they are flexible by size, AZ, and OS — if you change from m5.xlarge to m5.2xlarge or m6i.large, the savings plan still applies. This is the sweet spot between Standard RI savings (highest, but rigid on type/AZ) and Compute Savings Plan flexibility (lower savings but covers all families/regions/Lambda/Fargate).","distractors":{"A":"Standard RIs are locked to the specific instance type (m5.xlarge), AZ, and OS — changing to a different m-family instance requires modifying or selling the RI, adding operational complexity.","C":"Compute Savings Plans provide flexibility across ALL instance families, regions, Lambda, and Fargate (up to 66% savings) — but since this company only needs flexibility within the m-family in one region, EC2 Instance Savings Plans provide higher savings (up to 72%) for the same constraint.","D":"Convertible RIs allow changing to any instance type of equal or greater value within the same region (up to 54% savings) — less savings than EC2 Instance Savings Plans for this use case."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/savingsplans/latest/userguide/what-is-savings-plans.html","tags":["savings-plans","reserved-instances","ec2","cost","flexibility"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-e1f2g3h4","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Resilient Architectures","topic":"Disaster Recovery","difficulty":"hard","question":"A company requires an RTO of 15 minutes and an RPO of 5 minutes for their production database. They currently use RDS Multi-AZ. Does Multi-AZ alone meet these requirements, and if not, what additional configuration is needed?","options":{"A":"Yes — RDS Multi-AZ provides near-zero RPO (synchronous replication) and ~1-2 minute RTO, which meets both requirements","B":"No — Multi-AZ RTO is 15-30 minutes. Add a read replica in a second region to meet the 15-minute RTO","C":"No — Multi-AZ only replicates within one AZ. Enable cross-region replication to meet RPO requirements","D":"Yes — but only if the Multi-AZ standby is in a different region, not just a different AZ"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RDS Multi-AZ uses synchronous replication — the primary commits a transaction only after the standby acknowledges it, resulting in effectively zero RPO (no data loss). Automatic failover to the standby typically completes in 1-2 minutes, well within the 15-minute RTO requirement. Multi-AZ alone meets both the 15-minute RTO and 5-minute RPO requirements — in fact, it exceeds them significantly.","distractors":{"B":"Multi-AZ RTO is 1-2 minutes, not 15-30 minutes — a read replica in another region would add cross-region failover capability but is not needed to meet these specific requirements.","C":"Multi-AZ replicates synchronously to the standby in a DIFFERENT AZ (same region) — 'only replicates within one AZ' is factually incorrect.","D":"Multi-AZ standby is in a different AZ within the same region by definition — cross-region Multi-AZ is not a standard RDS feature (Aurora Global Database provides cross-region capability)."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZSingleStandby.html","tags":["rds","multi-az","rto","rpo","disaster-recovery"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-e1f2g3h5","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"High-Performing Architectures","topic":"Caching and ElastiCache","difficulty":"hard","question":"A leaderboard application writes player scores to ElastiCache Redis using a Sorted Set (ZADD). The leaderboard serves 100,000 reads/second. During a Redis cluster restart, all data is lost and the leaderboard shows empty until scores are rebuilt. How can data survive a Redis restart?","options":{"A":"Enable Multi-AZ with automatic failover — failover prevents data loss during restarts","B":"Enable Redis persistence (RDB snapshots and/or AOF) on the ElastiCache cluster","C":"Use Memcached instead of Redis — Memcached persists data to disk automatically","D":"Write scores to both Redis and DynamoDB; on Redis restart, rebuild the leaderboard from DynamoDB"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The most resilient architecture for a leaderboard is dual-write: scores are written to both Redis (for fast sorted set operations) and DynamoDB (as the durable source of truth). If Redis loses data, the application rebuilds the leaderboard from DynamoDB. Redis persistence (RDB/AOF) can also help, but Redis is fundamentally an in-memory store — ElastiCache RDB snapshots may lose up to minutes of data. The dual-write pattern ensures authoritative data in DynamoDB with Redis as a high-performance cache layer.","distractors":{"A":"Multi-AZ failover promotes a replica when the primary fails — but if the entire cluster is restarted or if a replica lags, data can still be lost. Multi-AZ prevents AZ-level failures, not data loss during restarts.","B":"ElastiCache Redis does support RDB snapshots and AOF persistence — enabling these would reduce data loss, but RDB snapshots have gaps between snapshots (potential data loss) and AOF can impact performance. DynamoDB as source of truth is more robust.","C":"Memcached does NOT persist data to disk — it is a pure in-memory cache with no persistence mechanism. This answer is factually incorrect."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/red-ug/RedisAOF.html","tags":["elasticache","redis","persistence","leaderboard","dynamodb","dual-write"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-e1f2g3h6","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Secure Architectures","topic":"IAM roles and cross-account","difficulty":"hard","question":"Company A (account 111111111111) wants to allow Company B (account 222222222222) to read objects from Company A's S3 bucket. Which combination is needed for cross-account S3 access?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM user in Account A and share the access keys with Account B","B":"In Account A: add a bucket policy allowing s3:GetObject from Account B (principal: 222222222222). In Account B: create an IAM role/user with permission to assume or directly call S3 in Account A","C":"Create VPC Peering between Account A and Account B to enable private S3 access","D":"Enable S3 Cross-Region Replication from Account A to Account B's bucket"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cross-account S3 access requires permissions granted from BOTH sides: (1) The resource-based bucket policy in Account A must explicitly allow Account B's principal (account, role, or user). (2) Account B's principal must have an IAM policy allowing s3:GetObject on the Account A bucket. With both in place, Account B's users/roles can access the bucket. For IAM roles, Account B creates a role and assumes it — the role's IAM policy plus the bucket policy together grant access.","distractors":{"A":"Sharing IAM user access keys across account boundaries is a security anti-pattern — it creates unrotatable, long-lived credentials with no IAM role separation.","C":"VPC Peering enables network-level connectivity between VPCs — S3 is a global service accessed via public endpoints or VPC Gateway Endpoints, not through VPC Peering.","D":"CRR copies objects to another bucket — it does not grant Account B access to read objects from Account A's original bucket."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-bucket-policies.html","tags":["iam","s3","cross-account","bucket-policy","trust-policy"],"verified":true},{"id":"07-e1f2g3h7","version":1,"setId":"set-07","domain":"Cost-Optimized Architectures","topic":"Cost allocation and visibility","difficulty":"medium","question":"A company wants to identify which specific EC2 Auto Scaling groups are generating the most cost, but their AWS Cost Explorer only shows aggregate EC2 costs without identifying individual Auto Scaling groups. What is the simplest solution?","options":{"A":"Enable AWS Cost Explorer hourly granularity","B":"Apply cost allocation tags (e.g., 'autoscaling-group-name') to instances launched by each Auto Scaling group, then activate the tag in Cost Explorer","C":"Create a separate AWS account for each Auto Scaling group","D":"Use AWS X-Ray to trace cost at the Auto Scaling group level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cost allocation tags applied to EC2 instances allow Cost Explorer to group and filter costs by tag. Auto Scaling groups can propagate tags to launched instances — add a tag like \`Name\` or \`asg-name: production-api\` to the Auto Scaling group's tag configuration with 'Tag New Instances' enabled. After activating the tag as a cost allocation tag in the Billing console (takes up to 24 hours to appear in Cost Explorer), you can filter and group EC2 costs by ASG.","distractors":{"A":"Hourly granularity shows cost over time at finer resolution but does not break down costs by resource (Auto Scaling group) without tags.","C":"Creating separate accounts per ASG is extreme overkill — it creates massive operational overhead for a cost visibility problem solvable with tags.","D":"AWS X-Ray traces application requests for performance analysis — it has no cost allocation or billing visibility functionality."},"source_url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/ec2/userguide/ec2-auto-scaling-tagging.html","tags":["cost-allocation-tags","auto-scaling","cost-explorer","visibility","ec2"],"verified":true}]`);export{e as default};